update lr_explanation_choice_notes t set body=v.nb from (values
('445761f0-a033-44dd-8f8a-e9fe3f314c29'::uuid,'961f46d57ad6f70f8eddb4042696eaea','The argument is about medical necessity, not ethics. Negate A: a dangerous operation can be ethically justified even when the condition it treats is not more dangerous. The researcher can accept that and still say some of these polyp removals were not medically necessary, so the argument survives.'),
('e67dd86c-ba1d-401c-ba7a-3776c1f4d704'::uuid,'b68bd82bbd7e369a41dae6fe4afde755','The evidence says some removed polyps turn out not to be malignant. The conclusion says some of the operations were not medically necessary. B is the bridge between those two terms: removing a nonmalignant polyp is not always medically necessary.

Negate it: surgical removal of nonmalignant polyps detected by the test is always medically necessary. Now every operation the test prompts was needed, malignant or not, and the conclusion has nothing left to stand on. The negation wrecks the argument, so the argument requires B.

Notice the soft wording. "Not always" only asks for some unnecessary removals, which is exactly as much as "can prompt" in the conclusion needs. On a necessary assumption question, a modest answer that connects the evidence''s term (not malignant) to the conclusion''s term (not medically necessary) is the shape to look for.'),
('aa811b8e-a357-4266-a385-36a5328c0e36'::uuid,'375b8b86439fd66d2e8564f99f009742','C tells us what to do about the test: stop using it or change it. The argument stops earlier, at the claim that the test can prompt unnecessary operations. Negate C: even if the test encourages unnecessary operations, it should still be used as is. The conclusion is untouched, because it never said what to do next.'),
('9b3bb964-6578-4b8e-9880-6cbdc1c8948e'::uuid,'bc20d17faa504007635282dc23ca7d39','D is about malignant polyps, and the gap is about the ones that are not malignant. Negate it: some malignant polyp found by the test should not be removed. That tells us nothing about whether removing a nonmalignant polyp is necessary, so the argument is unaffected.'),
('fdb97b9a-5be5-4229-a810-01eed8999bc1'::uuid,'387abc0446e8ea05fb662a9f3236d967','The argument never measures how useful the test is. Negate E: the test is medically useful even when it finds polyps that need no treatment. The researcher can agree and still say the test can prompt unnecessary surgery. "Only when" makes E a strong claim the argument has no use for.'),
('fb81a85d-efc2-4013-8e8e-475141d39573'::uuid,'6be9b36f3b2cd4ba77b4095ae1812c15','The argument already has its literary tablet: one translated tablet appears to contain literary writing. Negate A: most of the untranslated tablets do not contain literature. That one literary tablet is still there, so the challenge stands. More literary tablets would help, but the argument doesn''t need them.'),
('f5bd9ec2-65c5-4bda-af59-74139fd9e2f6'::uuid,'540ee70b237f96f94c79467a5eb0a500','B links tax records to other kinds of writing, but the argument never reasons from the tax records. It has direct evidence: a tablet that appears to contain literary writing. Negate B: some civilization kept tax records and nothing else. The argument loses nothing, because it rests on the literary tablet.'),
('074cc8b5-fcb8-4e6c-89c5-0507d8cd9401'::uuid,'71c79a98e514bc1ee68f08052c964120','C fills the gap we predicted. The evidence dates the literary tablet to between 3300 and 3200 B.C. The conclusion says this challenges the belief that the Sumerians were first. That only works if historians don''t already place Sumerian literature earlier than the tablet.

Negate C: historians generally believe the Sumerians did create literature earlier than 3300 B.C. Then the Egyptian tablet comes second on the historians'' own timeline, and it challenges nothing. The argument falls apart, so C is required.

The move to repeat: when the conclusion brings in something the evidence never mentioned (here, the Sumerians), the assumption usually connects that new term to the evidence. Ask what must be true about the new thing for the evidence to matter.'),
('07960647-5b03-4694-9b3e-62e2f4c098a7'::uuid,'4bc0330cad87c851ff2320b97eea2071','D would hurt the argument, not hold it up. Negate it: no historians are skeptical about the tablets'' authenticity. That only makes the evidence look better. A necessary assumption is something whose denial damages the argument, and denying D helps it.'),
('c8acb044-6f0a-4456-a01e-f6eaa7094bb2'::uuid,'b3998989ba890c5cf5d46bc0dc72d1b1','E dates when Sumerian civilization arose, but the belief in question is about when the Sumerians first created literature. A civilization can exist for centuries before anyone writes literature. Negate E: the civilization arose at some other time, say 4000 B.C. The argument still works as long as Sumerian literature came after the tablet.'),
('95b813da-ad0e-4942-9f81-60b5d11defc3'::uuid,'ed8091ad012201688a86f76d39d7ed56','A compares leatherbacks with other large aquatic reptiles. The surprise is a comparison between captive leatherbacks and wild ones. Fewer diseases attacking leatherbacks would be true in both places, so it creates no difference between captivity and the wild that could offset the captivity diseases.'),
('1bf3ba6b-8337-4212-bff8-746ce5757312'::uuid,'278d6840af6718315a8b26762d1ac973','B compares sea turtles with other marine animals, which is the wrong comparison. Even if sea turtles live long in general, that is true of captive and wild leatherbacks alike, so it doesn''t explain why the extra diseases in captivity fail to lower the captive odds.'),
('86782073-ac68-4ac1-9494-90dac1b164f1'::uuid,'69abc902fb9ab7ed173e878e384ea73c','This is the something-bad-about-the-wild answer we predicted. Captive leatherbacks face diseases the wild ones never meet. Wild leatherbacks face predators, and C tells us predators kill most of the wild ones that perish. Captive turtles are presumably safe from predators. So each setting has its own main danger, and the odds of reaching full life expectancy can come out about the same.

The move to repeat: when two groups end up equal even though one has an obvious disadvantage, look for a disadvantage on the other side that balances it.'),
('6629b443-0328-4197-ab72-cbc19c170594'::uuid,'db1ea5b45e96e054f9d17d0a090a446f','D deepens the paradox. If few zoologists know how to build an environment that is good for captive leatherbacks, captivity sounds even worse for them. That is one more reason to expect captive turtles to die younger, which is the opposite of what we need.'),
('a6695012-35bf-4641-b9ab-e8501b6b6f52'::uuid,'f080be4c342f511c5fd3b3f19b1f5866','E might tempt you if you suspect the life-expectancy figures are measured badly. But nothing in E says the error affects captive and wild turtles differently. If size is a poor guide to age everywhere, it gives no difference between the two settings, so the surprise remains.'),
('8431f89a-1c8e-4cef-83bf-891a618b49f4'::uuid,'81f6fe2f4470e158f847a3c18023bf42','A gives us a similarity: many of the same doctors work at Woodville and at the nearby hospitals. We need a difference that gives Woodville''s patients the benefit of private rooms. Shared doctors are the same on both sides, so they can''t explain why a hospital with only semiprivate rooms keeps up with hospitals that are mostly private.'),
('c6089d54-79b2-469d-b09e-cc79015781f3'::uuid,'c17b186ca100d3dc7a34d50aaf783f49','We want a reason Woodville''s semiprivate rooms do as well as other hospitals'' private rooms, and this gives it.

If Woodville puts one patient in each semiprivate room whenever it can, then in practice many of its patients get a room to themselves. So Woodville gets the benefits of private rooms without having any, and the surprise goes away.'),
('b31dce01-5a21-4d11-95a9-6bacb7aa43f9'::uuid,'9349fdf74abc01cfee45c7673ee62035','E does give a difference, location, but nothing ties being downtown to fewer infections or shorter stays. To make E work we''d have to invent that link ourselves, so the surprise is still standing.'),
('8ace9c8d-0dcb-4137-b6fe-d80674b2b36d'::uuid,'71b3c0b1d1e2b8d2af56ffc845705df8','A compares the two groups that didn''t get honey: cleaned-only wounds beat ointment wounds. That might explain why ointment came last, but it says nothing about honey. Honey still adds bacteria, and we still don''t know why honey wounds beat the cleaned-only ones.'),
('48fec1a8-becb-40f9-be8c-58f5125db904'::uuid,'7599ff4501156c01e31a7abd99773a97','B makes honey''s bacteria sound less dangerous, since there are fewer of them than in an infected wound. Fewer is still more than zero. At best honey hurts less than we feared; nothing in B makes it help. The second half, that ointment rarely kills all the bacteria, explains why ointment isn''t perfect, not why honey beats it.'),
('ff6dc1a3-f0d7-4571-bdfd-e1ad449f330f'::uuid,'e647665535e2ee61a8d965fe6bbbd854','C answers both comparisons we set up.

First half: honey inhibits the growth of bacteria in wounds, including the bacteria the honey contains. So honey wounds get less bacterial growth than wounds that were only cleaned. That explains why honey beat cleaning alone.

Second half: antibiotic ointments damage sensitive wound tissue, which slows healing. So the ointment pays a price that, as far as we''re told, honey doesn''t. That explains why honey beat the ointment.

The move to repeat: when a result beats more than one comparison group, check each answer against each group separately. The correct answer here has two parts because the surprise has two parts.'),
('e1db0eda-d40e-41d4-a5c8-e5b02526fd04'::uuid,'b55fb7eedcaa3219d51532afa166f1f3','D is the trap, because it gets halfway. The sugar in honey inhibits the growth of bacteria in wounds, including its own, which explains why honey wounds beat wounds that were only cleaned.

Now check the other comparison. The ointment kills bacteria too. D gives honey a bacteria advantage over plain cleaning, but no advantage over ointment, which already handles bacteria. Nothing in D explains why ointment wounds healed more slowly than honey wounds. C has this same first half plus the reason ointment falls behind.'),
('aaa983bb-69f4-4402-98e6-d532088837aa'::uuid,'b4e3573b3c709d0532b6d77810d493f2','E explains why ointment falls behind: it damages sensitive tissue. It also says honey doesn''t do that to cleaned wounds. But not causing tissue damage doesn''t make honey better than plain cleaning, and honey still brings in bacteria. E gives no reason honey wounds would heal faster than cleaned-only wounds.'),
('8d97cdea-36ea-4dd2-95b4-bd89a455d48e'::uuid,'88815b8b8dd9fe9c3097eaa91a70aed8','A compares Athens with political systems that came later. The puzzle is inside Athens: how did great work happen there during unrest? Knowing that later systems differ tells us nothing about what let Plato and Aristotle work through the turmoil.'),
('5b963036-0097-4c78-a61f-1aeaa43de10b'::uuid,'2a000870d162361f3f519552315dda2c','B is tempting because esteem sounds like encouragement. But the rule is about political stability, and B never connects esteem to instability. Citizens can admire thinkers in stable cities too. Ask: does B tell us what unrest normally takes away from thinkers, and how Athens kept it? It doesn''t, so the exception is still unexplained.'),
('114e83fd-ab48-4085-a287-8e874670d1ac'::uuid,'55b621adda66a53a650beee0aa2810ae','C does both jobs we predicted. First, it gives the reason the rule usually holds: in unstable political environments, financial support for intellectual endeavors is typically unavailable. Second, it shows why Athens escaped that: wealthy citizens provided the support anyway.

Now the rule and the counterexample fit together. Stability usually matters because of the money. Athens had the money without the stability, so Plato and Aristotle could keep working.

The move to repeat: to reconcile a general rule with an exception, look for an answer that explains why the rule normally holds and shows that the reason was missing in the exception.'),
('8e0e9495-513d-4b9a-9400-ac34ec7c33eb'::uuid,'6baf70077de9812cd933acdae621012c','D runs the other direction: advances sometimes lead to stability. That doesn''t explain how the advances happened in Athens while it was unstable. If anything it raises a new question, since Athens had unparalleled progress and still had great unrest.'),
('d7fec877-56bb-4d30-b311-5ae90dd30d8e'::uuid,'0a8b3400b82fd28f16dd84eb848802f5','E adds more Athenian thinkers to the list. That makes the counterexample bigger, not smaller: now a whole group did great work during unrest. We still have no reason why any of them could.'),
('8c79fefe-6411-48d4-86e0-698e02c11705'::uuid,'606fc1cf424c2da0df69c1971b56afd8','A tells us true camouflage would have to change with the seasons. Zebra stripes don''t change, so A only confirms that zebras aren''t camouflaged. It says nothing about how a zebra that stands out still survives.'),
('953c973f-0f8b-486d-a405-81877d62e2ae'::uuid,'18573c441aa5c0a7f8380cb1bc598def','B says zebras can tell which lions are preparing to hunt and ignore the others. That might save some wasted running, but it doesn''t make a zebra any harder to catch once a lion does hunt, and the lion still spots the zebra easily. Any prey animal could have this skill, so B gives no reason a zebra this visible gets away.'),
('1c6dd25f-9550-4d8a-84a4-f9839dfa1ca7'::uuid,'0a82200b659c3694f9ad7367c69b9c72','C makes things worse for zebras. Lions blend in with dry vegetation and can creep close before attacking. A hidden hunter going after easy-to-see prey should succeed more often, which deepens the puzzle about how zebras survive.'),
('65b2a7ca-2c58-4157-9cb4-d54c71f2cf74'::uuid,'f7c9225a8d5d6bd183991ab12a4c3864','D is about what lions do after a kill. Sharing the food doesn''t change how often lions bring down a zebra, and it has nothing to do with the stripes. The puzzle about surviving with vivid markings is untouched.'),
('e46b86bc-383f-4018-89f9-4eb554fc7f2f'::uuid,'0e87697392ef4451515010a7d82cfd5f','E gives the stripes a job other than hiding. Zebras generally run in a group when danger comes, and in a running group the stripes make it hard for a predator chasing one zebra to discern its outline. A lion can spot the herd easily but struggles to keep track of the one zebra it is chasing.

That resolves the puzzle. The stimulus showed the stripes don''t help a zebra hide. E shows they help it escape. Being seen and being caught are two different things, and E works in that gap.

The move to repeat: when the stimulus rules out the obvious benefit (here, camouflage), look for an answer where the same feature helps in another way.'),
('f6f341d7-2b02-40f0-bfca-1a390be7a6bc'::uuid,'36564e534c56aecb53385133655921aa','A explains why the rain''s diluting effect would be tiny: there isn''t much rainwater compared with the bay. A tiny dilution means pollution should stay about the same. But the pollution goes up, to its highest levels. A explains why the rain doesn''t help, not why pollution rises.'),
('003048d4-686d-424f-a90f-b313836a70bc'::uuid,'554010ecb46e56f5b13d4fb63400729a','B gives the connection we were looking for. Most of the rainwater that reaches Crystal Bay first falls on pesticide-treated fields. It picks up pesticides there and carries them into the bay. A heavy rainfall means a lot of runoff and a lot of pesticide arriving at once, so pollution peaks shortly after.

The move to repeat: this paradox quietly assumed the rain goes straight into the bay. When a fact seems to point one way (pure water should dilute), ask what happens between that fact and the result. Here the rain picks up pollution on the way.'),
('889a07ef-854a-4956-9c3f-ad86c48b8177'::uuid,'ba3493aadf8fd8bf320abcd2b329ebf6','C is about where the water in rain clouds comes from. Whatever its origin, the stimulus tells us the rain is almost totally pure. Where the water started gives no way for heavy rain to add pollution to the bay.'),
('54d91e1c-005a-4966-8f74-4f32c0c91758'::uuid,'1d17be1f0b354a92690ed4cc86f3d6f9','D names the bay''s leading pollution source, beachgoers'' trash that blows into the bay. But nothing in D ties that trash, or the wind that carries it, to rainfall. A pollution source with no stated link to rain can''t explain why the peak comes shortly after a heavy rainfall.'),
('e78d8afc-4e17-4531-8a75-7c639b296ff3'::uuid,'c0773d50d2c2802df2fda27cc1d55ed9','E says nearby ocean areas show an extremely similar pattern of pollutant increase and decrease. Suppose that pattern includes the same spike after rain. Then the puzzle has spread to more places, and we still don''t know why rain raises pollution anywhere. A repeated pattern is not an explanation of it.')
) v(id,ob,nb) where t.id=v.id and md5(t.body)=v.ob returning t.id;